  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How do stadium roofs stay up without pillars?",
      "a": "Many use a spoked-wheel design. Radial cables pulled tight between an inner tension ring and an outer compression ring hold the roof, and the outer ring sits on columns at the back of the stands.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a cable roof work?",
        "Why are there no pillars in stadiums?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do the radial cables do?",
      "a": "They are the spokes of the wheel, running from the outer ring to the inner ring. Pulled tight, they carry the weight of the roof covering and resist wind and snow.",
      "asks": [
        "What are the cables on a stadium roof?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a tension ring?",
      "a": "The tension ring is the inner hub of a spoked roof, usually made of steel cables. The radial cables pull outward on it from all sides, so it is stretched, like the hub of a bicycle wheel.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the inner ring of a stadium roof?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a compression ring?",
      "a": "The compression ring is the stiff outer rim of a spoked roof. The cables all pull it inward, squeezing it all round, so it must be strong in compression, like the rim of a bicycle wheel.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the outer ring of a stadium roof?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are stadium roof coverings made of?",
      "a": "Often PTFE-coated glass-fibre fabric or ETFE plastic cushions. They are very light, let some daylight through for the grass, and last for decades.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a stadium roof made of?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A spoked-wheel roof works like a bicycle wheel: cable spokes in tension pull an outer ring into compression and an inner hub into tension.",
    "PTFE-coated glass-fibre fabric, used on many stadium roofs, can last for several decades."
  ],
